## 5. Show timestamp mismatches, if any

```sql
WITH ctr_latest AS (
    SELECT
        contact_id,
        MAX(last_update_timestamp) AS expected_timestamp
    FROM public.ctr
    WHERE contact_id IS NOT NULL
    GROUP BY contact_id
),
flattened AS (
    SELECT
        contact_id,
        MAX(last_update_timestamp) AS actual_timestamp
    FROM public.ctr_flattened
    WHERE contact_id IS NOT NULL
    GROUP BY contact_id
)
SELECT
    c.contact_id,
    c.expected_timestamp AS original_latest_timestamp,
    f.actual_timestamp AS flattened_timestamp,
    DATEDIFF(
        second,
        c.expected_timestamp,
        f.actual_timestamp
    ) AS timestamp_difference_seconds
FROM ctr_latest c
JOIN flattened f
    ON c.contact_id = f.contact_id
WHERE c.expected_timestamp <> f.actual_timestamp
ORDER BY ABS(
    DATEDIFF(
        second,
        c.expected_timestamp,
        f.actual_timestamp
    )
) DESC;
```

A positive difference means the flattened timestamp is later. A negative difference means flattened is behind.

